<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Floriano Peixoto</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1839">1839</a>–<a href="/years/1895">1895</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Floriano Peixoto was a Brazilian army marshal who served as the second President of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a> from 1891 to 1894, taking over when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deodoro-da-fonseca">Deodoro da Fonseca</a> resigned. Known as the Iron Marshal, he crushed the Federalist Revolution in the south and a naval revolt in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-de-janeiro">Rio de Janeiro</a>, securing the new republic. He handed power to Prudente de Morais, the first civilian president.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/floriano-peixoto.jpg" alt="Floriano Peixoto" width="320" height="479" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Governo do Brasil · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Floriano_Peixoto_(1891).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Floriano Vieira Peixoto was born on 30 April 1839 in the province of Alagoas and moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-de-janeiro">Rio de Janeiro</a> at the age of sixteen to finish his secondary education. In 1858 he entered the Military School of Rio de Janeiro, graduating as a second lieutenant in 1861. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paraguayan-war">Paraguayan War</a> he served in the 1st Battalion of Homeland Volunteers, later as its commander, and took part in the battles of Tuyutí, Ytororó, Lomas Valentinas and Angostura.</p><p>He returned to the capital in 1870 and took a bachelor's degree in physical and mathematical sciences. Promoted marshal in 1874, he served for just over a year from 1884 as president of the province of Mato Grosso. At the Proclamation of the Republic he refused to lead imperial resistance to the coup, became Minister of War in 1890 and was elected vice president in February 1891.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>Peixoto rose to the presidency in November 1891, after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deodoro-da-fonseca">Deodoro da Fonseca</a> resigned amid a severe political crisis and an economic crisis worsened by the collapse of the Encilhamento bubble. His policies ended the successive economic crises of the years since 1889, and the economy stabilised and grew again. In 1892 a group of senior officers published the Manifesto of the 13 Generals, arguing that the constitution required him to call new elections rather than finish Deodoro's term.</p><p>In 1893 the Second Naval Revolt and the Federalist Revolution broke out, the latter in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-grande-do-sul">Rio Grande do Sul</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santa-catarina">Santa Catarina</a> and Paraná. Peixoto put both down by force, which earned him the nickname Iron Marshal and increased the centralisation of power. His supporters formed the cult known as Florianism, the first significant political movement in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a> centred on a republican figure. He left the presidency on 15 November 1894.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Peixoto died at Barra Mansa, in the state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-de-janeiro">Rio de Janeiro</a>, on 29 June 1895, from chronic liver disease, most often identified by historians as cirrhosis. He had been seriously ill for months. He is also called the Consolidator of the Republic. Views of his rule were sharply divided at the time; later generations of Brazilians came to regard it as necessary, and preferable to a continuing cycle of civil war. Nossa Senhora do Desterro, the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santa-catarina">Santa Catarina</a>, was renamed Florianópolis after the defeat of the federalist rebels there.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Floriano Peixoto” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After the military presidencies of Deodoro and <b>Floriano Peixoto</b>, civilian elites took control.</blockquote><a href="/state/first-brazilian-republic">First Brazilian Republic</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Maceió, in what is now <a href="/countries/brazil">Brazil</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-de-janeiro">Rio de Janeiro</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/brazil">Brazil</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=-9.67,-35.74&amp;year=1875" aria-label="Maceió on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Floriano Peixoto"><image href="/maps/globes/g33000.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4517 5823h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4517 5823h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=-9.67,-35.74&amp;year=1875">What was here in 1875? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
